page('5. Datastores, migration safety and scheduled work')
for name,text in [('PostgreSQL','Learning PostgreSQL runs one StatefulSet with a headless Service and PVC. Bootstrap SQL creates four databases and separate login roles, revokes public database connection and grants access only to the owning role. The superuser Secret is available to bootstrap/backup, not application Deployments. TLS server certificates are issued by cert-manager and verified by Node clients using the public CA ConfigMap. Production databases/roles must be provisioned through the managed provider before migration Jobs run.'),('Redis','Learning Redis runs one StatefulSet with append-only persistence and an authenticated configuration file written into /tmp. Generated learning passwords are alphanumeric; the password is not placed in Helm values or startup arguments. Applications receive it by Secret key. Production expects rediss:// with provider CA validation. Redis contains derived/non-authoritative data but rate limiting and event counts still need an appropriate availability/backup design.'),('Kafka','Learning Kafka uses one broker/controller in KRaft mode, a headless Service and PVC. Internal PLAINTEXT is restricted by namespace network policies; this learning arrangement is not accepted by the production profile. Production requires external TLS/SASL brokers, configured topic replication/retention and service-specific ACLs. The broker advertised address must be routable from client Pods; DNS configuration is part of deployment configuration.'),('Elasticsearch','Learning Elasticsearch runs one authenticated single-node StatefulSet, not a production cluster. A periodic catalog synchronization builds its index. Production uses HTTPS, restricted credentials, resilient storage/replicas and a capacity policy. Index data can be rebuilt from the catalog; protect credentials and monitor index freshness.'),('Migration Jobs','Each database service has ordered SQL files and a schema_migrations table. A PostgreSQL advisory lock serializes schema changes for that database. Each file runs in a transaction and is recorded only after commit. Job names incorporate the release version, have retry/deadline limits and run before application wave 2 in Argo CD. Readiness checks the service table so traffic is not served against an absent schema.'),('CronJobs','Notification digest counts queues with SCAN, avoids KEYS and does not print messages. Video encoding remains a bounded simulation. Learning PostgreSQL backup writes a full SQL dump with restrictive permissions, retains seven days and requires a restore exercise. A production backup must live outside the cluster failure domain, be encrypted and support documented RPO/RTO.')]:heading(name,2);para(text)
page('6. Security, TLS and dynamic configuration')
para('Service discovery is dynamic at the Kubernetes level: stable Service DNS names are rendered from release namespace and cluster domain, while endpoint Pod IPs change automatically. External managed endpoints and the public hostname still need environment configuration once. The browser never asks a viewer to enter a service URL, password, database host or token.')
para('The learning secret bootstrap generates cryptographically random credentials and sends Secret JSON through kubectl stdin. It does not print secret values, persist a plaintext .env file in the repository or rotate existing database passwords unexpectedly. Production uses External Secrets backed by provider workload identity. Base64 is not encryption; API/etcd encryption, audited access, backup protection and cluster policy remain platform responsibilities.')
para('TLS is implemented in separate layers: ingress certificates protect browser transport; verified PostgreSQL SSL protects DB connections; production external Redis/Elasticsearch/Kafka use TLS; Istio STRICT mTLS protects application service traffic when mesh proxies and CNI are actually installed. A Certificate object or a PeerAuthentication object alone cannot prove encrypted transport.')
para('The learning root CA private key remains in cert-manager. trust-manager reads that source in its configured trust namespace and propagates only public CA bytes into labeled namespaces. Application containers mount the public ConfigMap; they cannot request or list Secrets through their ServiceAccounts. Import the public learning CA into Windows/browser trust for HTTPS; a real domain should use ACME or organizational PKI.')
para('NetworkPolicies model explicit request edges, DNS, ingress and metrics. Use Calico/Cilium to enforce them; kindnet does not enforce the network policy contract. External provider IP/CIDR changes and Kubernetes API DNAT behavior need environment-specific rules and tests. Mesh ingress must be configured so the ingress controller reaches strict backends over mTLS.')
para('Secret updates in environment variables require Pod restart. External Secrets refresh alone does not hot-reload DB credentials or signing keys. Plan database role updates, dual-key verification for JWT rotation, coordinated rollouts and CA rotation. This iteration fails closed on missing configuration but does not implement an automated end-to-end key-rotation system.')
page('7. Observability: logs, metrics, traces and alerts')
para('Node services emit structured request logs containing timestamp, level, service, request ID, normalized route and response status. They avoid passwords, raw cookies, authorization tokens and SQL contents. HTTP route labels avoid unbounded user/title cardinality. Prometheus client metrics include process counters, request totals and duration histograms; Python provides matching HTTP metrics and its recommendation counter.')
para('ServiceMonitors select application Services and scrape /metrics. PrometheusRule resources cover HTTP error fraction, repeated restarts and Deployments with zero available replicas. Resource dashboards rely on kube-state-metrics, node exporter and kubelet/cAdvisor. metrics-server supplies HPA and kubectl top, not Prometheus historical dashboards; these are different data paths.')
para('Node auto-instrumentation runs before service imports and traces HTTP/Express/Postgres/Redis/Kafka-supported libraries through OTLP HTTP. Python explicitly instruments Flask, Requests and Redis. Tempo must expose its OTLP receiver on the configured port and the NetworkPolicy must allow export. A realistic verification is a request trace across gateway → service → datastore, not merely a successful synthetic trace upload.')
para('The learning cluster uses Loki 3 TSDB v13 filesystem storage and Grafana Alloy; Fluent Bit and Promtail are not installed in the new cluster. Log ingestion from all six active platform namespaces has been verified.')
para('Alloy API discovery labels namespace, Pod, container and service. A single API collector does not need hostPath access or placement on every tainted worker. Production uses scoped RBAC, clustered collectors, authenticated Loki access/object storage and retention/capacity/recovery controls. Platform checks passed all 28 Prometheus targets, Grafana HTTPS trust and synthetic trace retrieval from Tempo. Real application traces and external Alertmanager notification delivery still need verification after application deployment.')
code('{namespace="streamflix-v2", service="catalog-service"}\nsum(rate(http_requests_total{namespace="streamflix-v2"}[5m]))\nhistogram_quantile(0.95, sum by(le,service)(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{namespace="streamflix-v2"}[5m])))')
page('8. GitHub Actions, artifact promotion and deployment')
para('Active workflows now live in .github/workflows. Actions are pinned to verified commit SHAs, with the source version retained in comments. Pull-request validation has read-only permissions. Image publication uses Docker Hub with repository Actions secrets DOCKERHUB_USERNAME and DOCKERHUB_TOKEN. The namespace is derived from that username and the release SHA from the workflow context. BuildKit attaches SBOM and provenance metadata. The built-in GITHUB_TOKEN is used separately to propose the digest promotion PR; enable repository permission for Actions to create pull requests. The first source push publishes images; deployment is triggered separately after promotion and runner setup.')
para('Validation gates cover Node security/HTTP tests, Python dependency behavior, chart rendering, Secret references, production configuration failure, real Docker Postgres/Redis integration and desktop/mobile browser tests. Docker contexts are repository-root because each Node service uses the shared runtime and root lockfile. Images are scanned, accompanied by SBOM/provenance and recorded by digest.')
para('A single promotion job requires the complete twelve-image digest set, updates one image manifest and opens a reviewable pull request. This avoids parallel service jobs racing to update the same Git branch. Promotion-only changes do not trigger a new image publication loop. Failed validation/image scans prevent promotion.')
para('Deployment is a separately triggered, approved environment workflow on a dedicated self-hosted runner that can reach the cluster. GitHub-hosted runners cannot reach the local kind API. The runner should use a restricted short-lived identity; do not commit or upload a cluster-admin kubeconfig. Helm waits for workloads and Jobs, times out, rolls back failed resource upgrades and limits release history.')
para('Production environments need protected branches, reviewed workflow changes, required checks, environment approvals and controlled runner access. Dependency updates are prepared through Dependabot. The workflows have been statically validated and action references checked. The first GitHub run built images but failed the frontend vulnerability gate on bundled npm tooling. The local runtime-image hardening change remains unverified; no complete promoted application release has passed yet.')
page('9. Later Argo CD ownership')
para('The Argo CD generator creates a scoped AppProject and a single Application for the v2 chart and environment/image overlays. Source repository and destination namespace are explicit configuration. Cluster resource creation is not granted. Platform controllers, CA/Bundle resources, namespaces and secret-provider identity are prepared separately by platform administrators.')
para('The application uses sync waves: Secret/configuration prerequisites before datastores; migrations after datastores; application Deployments after successful migrations. HPA-controlled replica counts are ignored for Git drift. Automatic prune/self-heal restores the desired release configuration; it is not a database recovery mechanism.')
para('After enabling Argo CD, retire the direct Helm deployment writer for the same namespace/release. GitHub Actions should test/build/scan and propose digest promotion; Argo CD should reconcile the reviewed Git state. Two controllers continuously writing the same release create ownership and drift conflicts.')
code('python3 scripts/v2/render-argocd.py --repo https://github.com/YOUR_ORG/YOUR_REPO.git --namespace streamflix-v2 --profile learning > /tmp/streamflix-argocd.yaml')
page('10. Deployment runbook and verification')
para('The detailed executable setup sequence is maintained in docs/v2/DEPLOYMENT.md. This guide records how the deployment is designed; it does not claim v2 was already deployed. Deploy the application through the reviewed CI release process.')
for title,text in [('Prepare platform','Install/version-pin a policy-enforcing CNI, ingress, metrics-server, cert-manager/trust-manager, monitoring CRDs and VPA. Prepare learning CA/trust or production organizational/ACME issuer. Set namespace Pod Security and quota policy.'),('Prepare identities','Learning uses the isolated credential generator. Production connects a workload-identity SecretStore to the provider and provisions DB roles/databases, Kafka topic/ACLs and least-privilege search credentials. Test RBAC and secret readiness.'),('Publish reviewed images','Push reviewed source through GitHub Actions, verify checks/scans/attestations and merge the complete digest promotion PR. Keep production profile input in an approved environment overlay.'),('Deploy','Run the approved deployment workflow against the configured namespace. Wait for TLS Secret/trust bundle, datastores, transactional migrations and Deployment readiness. Inspect rollout status and failed Pod events; image-pull DNS must work on every scheduled node.'),('Open the browser','Learning HTTPS is https://streamflix-v2.local:9443 through the ingress port-forward. Trust the public learning CA, keep the exact public origin configured and verify Secure session cookies. Production uses a real DNS/load-balancer hostname.'),('Verify distributed flows','Run live-smoke.mjs with trusted TLS. Verify registration/login, ownership, catalog, list/progress persistence, acknowledged Kafka → Redis count, search, recommendation caching and logout. Inspect a real trace, matching request logs, ServiceMonitor targets and HPA metrics.'),('Rollback/recover','Rollback application resources only if the old version is compatible with current schema. SQL/data changes require migration strategy or restore. Test the backup restore procedure in a separate environment before depending on it.')]:heading(title,2);para(text)
page('11. Evidence, boundaries and next acceptance gates')
validation=out/'VALIDATION.md'
para(validation.read_text() if validation.exists() else 'Validation results are recorded separately in docs/v2/VALIDATION.md.')
para('Browser tests use mock APIs to test UI behavior. The real service integration harness and the post-deployment smoke harness test different boundaries. Passing static Helm checks does not prove scheduling, CNI enforcement, certificate trust, runtime database permissions, DNS connectivity or workload health in the actual cluster.')
para('Before a production release, require a passing real integration/CI run, successful staging deployment, cross-user authorization tests, TLS/mTLS verification, failure/recovery testing, load tests and documented capacity. Run restore drills, signing-key/credential rotation drills and policy-denial tests. Add content provider/CDN, adaptive media processing and payment entitlements before selling subscriptions.')
para('Known extension work includes persistent analytics beyond Redis deduplication retention, dead-letter handling, catalog administration/delete indexing, email verification/password recovery/MFA, revocable sessions, durable provider notifications, payment webhooks, real transcoding and media rights. These are documented so a reader can distinguish implemented safeguards from future integrations.')
page('12. Reference sources and regeneration')
